            <ArticleTitle>Neonatal myocardial infarction: case report and review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E97-102</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1747-0803.2012.00660.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Myocardial infarction in a neonate is rare. We describe the case of a full-term male who presented with respiratory distress. A chest radiograph demonstrated cardiomegaly. An electrocardiogram revealed ST segment changes suggestive of ischemia. Cardiac enzymes were elevated and an echocardiogram revealed a regional wall motion abnormality. Cardiac catheterization was performed demonstrating occlusion of the ramus intermedius branch of the left main coronary artery. The patient decompensated, requiring extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO). The infant was able to be decannulated from ECMO support in 5 days and was ultimately discharged on hospital day 25. We review this case as well as the literature on neonatal myocardial infarction.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2012 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
